    <!-- Challenge -->
<section
    id="challenge"
    class="project-section w3-padding-64"
    aria-labelledby="challenge-heading">

    <h2 id="challenge-heading">The challenge</h2>

        <p class="w3-large">
            Negotiation proficiency requires deliberate, repeated practice, but a
            traditional paired role-play may give each student only one opportunity
            to test an opening offer.
        </p>
        <p>
            Dr. Shirli Kopelman had successfully used a PDF-based negotiation case
            for an in-person role-play exercise for years. The challenge was to
            translate that case into an AI experience that would create authentic
            resistance, remain faithful to the case, and allow students to test
            different strategies under consistent conditions.
        </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Solution -->
<section
    id="solution"
    class="project-section w3-padding-32"
    aria-labelledby="solution-heading">

    <h2 id="solution-heading">The solution</h2>

        <p>
            I independently designed and built an AI negotiation partner in U-M
            Maizey using the faculty member’s existing case PDF as the source
            material.
        </p>

        <p>
            The bot understands the details of the case, follows realistic buyer
            logic, and maintains a consistent personality. It responds dynamically
            to students’ offers—for example, reacting negatively to an excessively
            high anchor or questioning whether an unusually low offer is serious.
        </p>

        <p>
            Unlike a human negotiation partner who remembers an earlier attempt,
            the bot treats every new conversation independently. Students can
            therefore test multiple strategies under consistent conditions and
            compare the results.
        </p>


    </section>

